A record of the input the compositor was given, and of the modifier state it then holds: for
finding a fault in the input by looking instead of guessing (docs/input-record.md). It is the
last of three places such a record can be written; the other two need no patch
(scripts/input_record.py: what reached the pipeline, and what the application was told).

NOT for the plugin a measurement or the gate uses. Build it in a folder of its own and name
that folder to a trial with ISTESSO_HOST_PLUGIN; the folders the other patches' headers name
stay as they are.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch,
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch, gst-wayland-display-windows.patch and
gst-wayland-display-dialogs.patch in this directory, in that order. The lines of context are
from that project and from those patches.

What it adds:

- wayland-display-core/src/comp/input_record.rs: when the environment names a file
  (ISTESSO_COMPOSITOR_INPUT_RECORD=/path), one JSON line is added to it for each key, button
  and scroll the compositor is given, with the keyboard's modifier state after it: the three
  numbers a `wl_keyboard.modifiers` event carries (depressed, latched, locked), the modifiers
  by name, and whether a window has the keyboard (a key is passed on only then). Without the
  variable nothing is opened and nothing is written.
- A modifier key is named (both Shifts, Controls, Alts and Supers, Caps Lock, Num Lock). Which
  key any other key was is never written down: its line says "key". No pointer position is
  written down. The file is its owner's only.
- comp/input.rs: `keyboard_input`, `pointer_button` and `pointer_axis` call it.

    {"kind":"header","record":"compositor","version":1,"note":"..."}
    {"t_ns":...,"kind":"modifier","name":"shift_l","state":"down","depressed":1,"latched":0,"locked":0,"held":["shift"],"focus":true}
    {"t_ns":...,"kind":"key","name":"key","state":"down","depressed":1,"latched":0,"locked":0,"held":["shift"],"focus":true}
    {"t_ns":...,"kind":"button","name":"left","state":"down","depressed":8,"latched":0,"locked":0,"held":["alt"],"focus":true}
    {"t_ns":...,"kind":"scroll","name":"vertical","dx":0.000,"dy":-0.375,"depressed":1,"latched":0,"locked":0,"held":["shift"],"focus":true}

How to build (on the host, in a folder of its own; nothing existing is touched):

    git clone https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display ~/.cache/istesso-inputlog-wip/gwd-input-record
    cd ~/.cache/istesso-inputlog-wip/gwd-input-record && git checkout 1301f86
    git apply <the four patches above, in that order> && git apply <this file>
    cargo test -p wayland-display-core input_record
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

and a trial takes it with

    ISTESSO_HOST_PLUGIN='~/.cache/istesso-inputlog-wip/gwd-input-record/target/release' ISTESSO_INPUT_RECORD=1 TRY=channel scripts/try.sh COMMAND

